1
00:00:02,600 --> 00:00:04,483
It always rains in this town.

2
00:00:04,953 --> 00:00:08,780
And somewhere out there, a story is looking for its next case.

3
00:00:10,130 --> 00:00:16,556
This is SCP-3143: Murphy Law in… The Foundation Always Rings Twice!

4
00:00:16,826 --> 00:00:21,725
A Euclid-class anomaly from the SCP Wiki, written by The Great Hippo.

5
00:00:23,075 --> 00:00:30,697
SCP-3143 is an intrafictional construct: a living, thinking story that can warp reality.

6
00:00:30,967 --> 00:00:39,668
When it activates, it flattens part of the real world into a movie script: a hard-boiled crime noir, straight out of the 1930s.

7
00:00:40,018 --> 00:00:47,607
Inside that script, SCP-3143 plays the lead: a private detective hired to crack a case.

8
00:00:47,777 --> 00:00:53,731
Anyone caught in the effect becomes a character, talking and acting like they walked out of a pulp novel.

9
00:00:53,901 --> 00:01:00,029
The story runs until the case is solved the way the genre demands. Then reality snaps back.

10
00:01:00,199 --> 00:01:06,068
Whatever happened in the story really happened. But the people involved usually remember none of it.

11
00:01:06,718 --> 00:01:08,957
The detective's name? Murphy Law.

12
00:01:09,277 --> 00:01:13,117
It's a pun on Murphy's Law: anything that can go wrong, will.

13
00:01:13,867 --> 00:01:15,909
Containing a story isn't easy.

14
00:01:16,079 --> 00:01:22,959
A Foundation bot called I/O-ISMETA scans online fiction communities for signs that Murphy is back.

15
00:01:23,129 --> 00:01:30,621
When he surfaces, a member of the Pataphysics Department, experts in the logic of fiction, is sent to deconstruct him.

16
00:01:30,791 --> 00:01:38,939
And Mobile Task Force Iota-10, the "Damn Freds," hunts for SCP-3143-A: the man who wrote him.

17
00:01:39,789 --> 00:01:47,794
SCP-3143 was first noticed in 2005, after a containment breach at Site-95.

18
00:01:48,014 --> 00:01:54,281
In 2012, the Pataphysics Department and the Department of Analytics finally made contact.

19
00:01:55,831 --> 00:02:04,720
In Interview 001, Dr. Thaddeus Thaum, a literature expert from the Pataphysics Department, walks into Murphy's office.

20
00:02:04,940 --> 00:02:10,918
Murphy narrates, draws his .44… and Thaum calmly points out that he can hear the narration.

21
00:02:11,188 --> 00:02:19,286
Then Thaum critiques the story to pieces. It isn't even proper noir, he says. No doomed anti-hero. No moral grey.

22
00:02:19,406 --> 00:02:24,131
Just "fantasy escapism dressed up in a suit, a hat, and a drinking habit."

23
00:02:24,501 --> 00:02:30,319
As the critique lands, the screenplay collapses into a plain transcript, and Murphy is gone.

24
00:02:30,589 --> 00:02:40,278
In his place sits SCP-3143-A: Murphy Lawden, a retired shoe salesman from New Jersey who lives alone with two cats.

25
00:02:40,448 --> 00:02:44,760
Years ago, he wrote an unfinished screenplay called It Always Rains.

26
00:02:46,110 --> 00:02:51,313
The next day, Lawden asks the question every writer dreads: was the script any good?

27
00:02:52,883 --> 00:02:54,523
"It was fairly awful."

28
00:02:56,473 --> 00:03:05,421
By Interview 018, Lawden is miserable. He's stopped eating, and his pencils have been taken away, because he keeps writing about Murphy.

29
00:03:05,641 --> 00:03:10,091
Why did the story have to stop? Murphy was helping people. It was fun.

30
00:03:10,461 --> 00:03:19,034
Then he turns the tables. The Foundation, he says, cloaks itself in the trappings of science: the terms, the titles, the interview logs.

31
00:03:19,104 --> 00:03:21,665
All to make its own story sound plausible.

32
00:03:22,035 --> 00:03:27,894
The transcript snaps back into a screenplay. The subject isn't Lawden anymore. It's Murphy.

33
00:03:28,164 --> 00:03:34,496
And he has a line for all of us: we're all just characters in that trashy dime-store novel called life.

34
00:03:34,766 --> 00:03:38,343
He tosses Thaum aside, lights a cigarette, and walks out.

35
00:03:40,493 --> 00:03:44,222
Then comes the twist, locked behind Level 4 clearance.

36
00:03:44,492 --> 00:03:53,891
In an email, Site Director August admits that Dr. Thaum and the Pataphysics Department were made up: a test run for a fictional countermeasure.

37
00:03:54,111 --> 00:03:57,980
Even Murphy Lawden and It Always Rains never existed.

38
00:03:58,250 --> 00:04:03,229
The Foundation tried to beat a story by writing a better one… and it didn't work.

39
00:04:03,549 --> 00:04:12,418
August orders everyone to leave SCP-3143 alone. They've learned a lot about him, but he's learned a lot about them, too.

40
00:04:12,638 --> 00:04:20,043
And one more thing: SCP-423, a creature that slips itself into written stories, has gone missing.

41
00:04:21,593 --> 00:04:24,985
Then, just like a movie, the file ends with credits.

42
00:04:25,455 --> 00:04:30,101
Dr. Thaum, played by Thaddeus Thaum. The task force, played by Fred.

43
00:04:30,571 --> 00:04:32,917
And Murphy Law… played by himself.

44
00:04:37,667 --> 00:04:40,330
So, what is SCP-3143?

45
00:04:40,700 --> 00:04:44,698
A detective who knows he's fictional, and suspects that you are, too.

46
00:04:44,968 --> 00:04:50,999
Every attempt to contain him is just another story. And Murphy Law is very good at stories.
